Semaphore + Concurrent API
________________________________________________________________
-Semaphore is a synchronization mechanism used to
 limit how many threads/requests can access a resource at the same time.

 Semaphore(5) → only 5 concurrent requests allowed
 Extra requests wait until a permit is released

 Semaphore sem = new Semaphore(3);
 Only 3 threads can run at same time.
 Others wait
 ----------------------------------------------------------------
 Where Industry Uses It
 Real Examples:
 Payment Gateway Calls
 Only 10 concurrent payment requests.
 Email Sending Service
 Max 20 emails at once.
 Image Processing API
 Prevent CPU overload.
 Database Connection Protection
 Limit queries to pool size.
 Microservices
 Stop traffic spikes crashing service.
 ------------------------------------------------------------
 Scenario:
 5 students want to use 2 computers in lab.
 Only 2 students can use computers at once.

 example:
import java.util.concurrent.Semaphore;

class Student extends Thread{

private Semaphore semaphore;
private int studentId;

public Student(Semaphore semaphore, int studentId){
this.semaphore=semaphore;
this.studentId=studentId;
}

@override
public void run(){
try{
sout("Student "+studentId + "waiting for computer..");
semaphore.acquire();//aquire permit
sout("studnet "+ studentId + "got computer..");
Thread.sleep(3000);//using computer
sout("student" + studentId + "finished work");
}
catch(Exception e){
e.printStackTrace();
}finally{
semaphore.release();// give permit back
}
}
}
public class Main{
psvm{
Semaphore semaphore=new Semaphore(2);// only 2 computers
for(int i=1;i<=5;i++){
new Student(semaphore, i).start();
}
}
}
------------------------------------------------
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    new Student(semaphore, i).start();
}
Creates 5 separate threads:
Student 1 thread
Student 2 thread
Student 3 thread
Student 4 thread
Student 5 thread
All run concurrently.
-----------------------------------------------------------
Why Same Semaphore Passed?
new Student(semaphore, i)
All students share same semaphore object.
So all compete for same 2 permits.
If each had separate semaphore:
new Semaphore(2)
then no control happens.
